But everyone came in.

21
00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:57.380
Hi, guys.

22
00:00:57.540 --> 00:00:57.780
Hi.

23
00:00:57.900 --> 00:00:58.480
Happy Saturday.

24
00:00:58.560 --> 00:00:59.940
Thanks for coming in on a Saturday, everyone.

25
00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:01.800
Happy Saturday to you as well.

26
00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:05.360
Today, we are talking about Secretary's Day.

27
00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:11.820
It is Season 6, Episode 22, written by Mindy Kaling and directed by Steve Carell.

28
00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:13.080
So exciting.

29
00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:14.280
That's a great duo.

30
00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:15.760
Would you like a summary?

31
00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:17.080
More than anything.

32
00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:18.020
All right.

33
00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:19.180
Here it is.

34
00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:23.300
Andy tries to make Secretary's Day special for Aaron.

35
00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:26.460
He encourages Michael to take Aaron to lunch.

36
00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:34.540
During which, Michael accidentally reveals Andy's past engagement to Angela, something Aaron did not know about.

37
00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:44.060
Meanwhile, a spoof video circulates likening Kevin to Cookie Monster, which Gabe finds offensive, and he flexes his authority to squash it.

38
00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:46.580
I love that you called it a spoof video.

39
00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:49.980
What would it be?

40
00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:52.240
Maybe like a parody video?

41
00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:54.420
Are they not the same?

42
00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:57.860
I think they are, but it's just funny to me.

43
00:01:58.000 --> 00:01:59.800
Spoof video sounds funny to me.

44
00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:04.960
Well, I want to tell you all, we do not have fast facts today.

45
00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:05.800
None.

46
00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:06.860
No fast facts.

47
00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:08.140
You want to know why?

48
00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:08.740
Why?

49
00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:09.380
I'll tell you.

50
00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:20.440
While Angela had food poisoning, by the way, you'll hear it in her voice.

51
00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:21.540
Ellie knew right away.

52
00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:22.040
Here's the thing.

53
00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:25.000
We had to do it over Zoom because I had to be by a bathroom.

54
00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:26.340
Sorry, TMI.

55
00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:28.920
And Ellie looked at me right away.

56
00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:31.240
I hadn't even said a word, and she goes, are you okay?

57
00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:32.300
Yeah.

58
00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:37.300
But you guys, we were so excited to talk to Ellie, and I didn't want to miss it.

59
00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:40.400
So here is our interview with Ellie.

60
00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:41.380
She's the beSt.

61
00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:44.220
Hi, Ellie.

62
00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:45.300
Hello, Ellie.

63
00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:47.260
Hi, Jenna and Angela.

64
00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:48.100
How are you?

65
00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:48.900
Good.

66
00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:50.280
Welcome to Office, ladies.

67
00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:51.520
Welcome, welcome.

68
00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:52.760
Hello.

69
00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:55.780
I am so happy to be here.

70
00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:58.340
I am a huge fan of your show.

71
00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:02.840
I'm a huge fan of the two of you, and it is always delightful chatting with you.

72
00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:04.360
So thank you for having me.

73
00:03:04.820 --> 00:03:07.820
Ellie, you are a ray of sunshine that I can really use right now.

74
00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:13.220
As Jenna knows, I have a little bit of food poisoning, and you're my first thing back from

75
00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:13.820
food poisoning.

76
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:14.220
Okay.

77
00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:14.900
So congratulations.

78
00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:21.340
I hope I don't send you back into illness, but I'm sorry that you're...

79
00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:23.780
I hope that you are feeling better, Angela.

80
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:24.820
You are a trooper.

81
00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:29.040
I know you're not asking for compliments, but I'm here to tell you you're a trooper.

82
00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:30.680
So I'm glad that you're feeling a little better.

83
00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:31.520
I'll take them today.

84
00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:32.220
I'll take it.

85
00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:37.940
Well, Ellie, we always start our interviews with asking each person how they got their job

86
00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:38.680
on the office.

87
00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:42.100
I got my job on the office in sort of a roundabout way.

88
00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:50.320
I met with Greg Daniels and Mike Schur at the beginning of 2008, maybe, when they were developing

89
00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:51.640
Parks and Recreation.

90
00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:58.020
And I believe, although I was never explicitly told that I was auditioning for Parks and Recreation,

91
00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:03.860
like there was an untitled script that I read from in a room with Greg and Allison Jones.

92
00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:05.700
Nothing ever came of it.

93
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:11.060
The characters didn't have any names that matched the ones on what was to become Parks and Recreation.

94
00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:15.280
So I really don't know what I was doing in that room with them, but that's how I met them.

95
00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:23.240
Then later that, you know, like a few months later, there was an audition when Pam went to go work

96
00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:25.240
at the Michael Scott Paper Company.

97
00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:28.800
There was an opening for a temporary secretary.

98
00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:35.080
And so since I had already met some of those, you know, big guys from the office, it was two guys.

99
00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:35.860
That's why I said big guys.

100
00:04:36.460 --> 00:04:39.500
I was, I guess, on there in their world, in their orbit.

101
00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:41.940
So I came in to read for that part.

102
00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:43.400
I read with Ed HelMs.

103
00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:47.480
And then I got the call, you know, the next week that I had booked the part.

104
00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:49.880
So that's how I got on the office, you guys.

105
00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:50.660
Wow.

106
00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:51.940
That is so wild.

107
00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:52.860
You know what, though?

108
00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:55.760
So I remember like showrunners would do that.

109
00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:59.080
If they had a project, they didn't want it leaked out in any way.

110
00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:03.240
You would go in sometimes and read and not really know much about what you were reading for.

111
00:05:04.420 --> 00:05:05.920
And that's kind of how you met Greg.

112
00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:07.740
You were, I'm sure that was Parks and Rec.

113
00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:09.060
You know, it sounds like it.

114
00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:09.980
It had to have been.

115
00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:11.500
I don't know what part it was.

116
00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:13.860
Like I never said they wanted to keep everything a little tight-lipped.

117
00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:18.400
But, but yeah, I mean, you guys, it goes without saying.

118
00:05:18.700 --> 00:05:20.580
So that was, that was the end of season five.

119
00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:21.720
I think that I came on.

120
00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:24.100
I was such a fan of the office.

121
00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:27.080
So it's like, I mean, I watched it with my family.

122
00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:28.840
I watched it, you know, by myself.

123
00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:37.580
It was surreal actually meeting you guys for the first time because I was so familiar with the show and felt like I already knew you.

124
00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:39.940
You played it so cool though, Ellie.

125
00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:40.340
I know.

126
00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:41.700
I would have never known.

127
00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:44.200
You were super like, hey, what's up?

128
00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:45.660
You weren't, you didn't go crazy.

129
00:05:45.980 --> 00:05:52.880
Oh, well, that is my finest performance because I was like, my heart inside was just like beating a million miles a minute.

130
00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:56.000
But with a sitcom, they're in your living room every week.

131
00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:57.500
You feel like you know them.

132
00:05:57.700 --> 00:06:02.420
I'm sure this happens to you all the time where people feel like they know you very well, even though you've never met them.

133
00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:04.880
And so I felt like I knew all of you.

134
00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:07.080
And yet you had never seen my face before.

135
00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:14.820
So it's a surreal experience of suddenly working alongside these people who you sort of consider to be your friends, but also icons.

136
00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:16.800
And so it was all of that at once.

137
00:06:16.820 --> 00:06:18.100
And it was a lot.

138
00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:20.760
So I'm really glad you thought I played it cool because I didn't feel cool.

139
00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:25.540
Now, originally, it was just supposed to be a few episodes, right?

140
00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:28.360
And then Pam was going to come back from Michael Scott Paper Company.

141
00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:35.280
And I remember discussions about whether or not Pam would return to the receptionist desk or do something else.

142
00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:38.620
And I had sat down with Greg and he asked me my opinion.

143
00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:42.220
He said, what do you think Pam would want?

144
00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:44.580
And I said, well, I don't know.

145
00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:54.120
After art school and this big move with Michael, I feel like it might be a little depressing if she's just back at reception again.

146
00:06:54.440 --> 00:06:57.760
I would love some movement for this woman.

147
00:06:58.760 --> 00:06:59.880
And he agreed.

148
00:07:00.040 --> 00:07:05.060
And he said, all right, I think we're going to make Pam part of the sales team.

149
00:07:05.060 --> 00:07:08.640
And we're going to move her to the desk clump with Dwight and Jim.

150
00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:14.300
And then that kind of opened the door for we need someone to sit at reception.

151
00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:17.860
And Ellie, you were so beloved by everyone.

152
00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:25.160
I mean, listen, there is a chance that Greg was making me feel like I had a part of that decision.

153
00:07:25.160 --> 00:07:28.640
But the real story was, listen, we're not letting Ellie Kemper go.

154
00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:33.720
So we're going to make you feel good by telling you I want Pam to move to sales.

155
00:07:34.060 --> 00:07:39.460
But because everyone was so excited for you to come on in a more permanent position.

156
00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:41.980
What was that part like from your end?

157
00:07:42.260 --> 00:07:50.840
Okay, Jenna, first of all, I have to say, first and foremost, thank you for your input that Pam should be promoted to salesperson.

158
00:07:50.840 --> 00:07:57.680
Because you gave me in real life a job with a paycheck on one of the most iconic shows in television history.

159
00:07:57.820 --> 00:07:58.560
So thank you.

160
00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:03.200
I did not give you gave yourself a job by kicking ass in that role.

161
00:08:03.580 --> 00:08:03.920
That's right.

162
00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:05.400
That's what got you that job.

163
00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:07.360
You won them over in the room, Ellie.

164
00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:08.240
You really did.

165
00:08:08.500 --> 00:08:10.640
And then you showed up and you crushed it.

166
00:08:10.940 --> 00:08:12.620
So you're the reason you were there.

167
00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:13.520
A hundred percent.

168
00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:14.760
Yeah, everyone is.

169
00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:15.640
I just more.

170
00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:16.160
I need you.

171
00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:22.020
I need to listen to you when I wake up every morning, like you need to be in my bathroom mirror saying, God, you can do it.

172
00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:22.540
You can do it.

173
00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:24.820
So thank you for those very, very encouraging words.

174
00:08:24.980 --> 00:08:31.040
What was that moment when they called you and they said, we're going to make this character a regular character?

175
00:08:31.300 --> 00:08:32.320
That was insane.

176
00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:38.540
So, yes, I think my the arc was at first four episodes, maybe, maybe six.

177
00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:42.160
But then it, you know, Aaron stayed for the rest of the season.

178
00:08:42.160 --> 00:08:43.720
And then it wasn't until June.

179
00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:47.600
So the show had stopped, I guess, the show and that season ended in May.

180
00:08:47.800 --> 00:08:50.080
And in June is when they called my agents.

181
00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:57.600
And then my agents called me to say that we want to offer her a, you know, a regular spot on the show.

182
00:08:57.740 --> 00:09:10.420
And that that is like you have won the lottery, of course, because I and which I do feel that I did, because not only creatively is the show just so spectacular.

183
00:09:10.420 --> 00:09:16.060
But I just felt so lucky to be working with normal people who didn't have there were no egos.

184
00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:23.500
There was I mean, you were there longer than I was, but I there was no like fighting or like drama.

185
00:09:23.500 --> 00:09:26.420
It felt like a very, very nice group of people to work with.

186
00:09:26.420 --> 00:09:30.420
And it was so I felt, again, like, OK, this is wow.

187
00:09:30.620 --> 00:09:32.300
This is my big break.

188
00:09:32.540 --> 00:09:35.840
And so that that was an exhilarating moment when I got that call.

189
00:09:36.020 --> 00:09:39.380
Our writer, Jean, told us that for your first episode.

190
00:09:39.660 --> 00:09:41.120
And this is so crazy, Ellie,

191
00:09:41.320 --> 00:09:51.320
that you were asked to perform the role of Aaron two ways in every scene because they were still trying to figure out exactly what they needed from your character.

192
00:09:51.320 --> 00:09:51.420
Sure.

193
00:09:51.780 --> 00:09:56.400
And so the one way we know her as right is just so adorable.

194
00:09:56.440 --> 00:10:00.400
And she just desperately wants Michael to love her and like her.

195
00:10:00.560 --> 00:10:01.380
And it's so cute.

196
00:10:01.580 --> 00:10:05.140
But I guess the other way was like a sarcastic version.

197
00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:06.420
Do you remember this?

198
00:10:06.580 --> 00:10:07.740
I do remember that.

199
00:10:07.880 --> 00:10:14.300
And I'm glad you brought that up because I do remember that specific direction, which was we haven't really figured this character out yet.

200
00:10:14.300 --> 00:10:19.360
But and so there were I don't know if it was every take, but there were moments where it was OK.

201
00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:26.640
They were just sort of, you know, I think adjusting and like modulating the character in terms of what she would be.

202
00:10:26.840 --> 00:10:39.920
And so there were a lot of takes where it was just more straightforward and a little bit, you know, I guess I don't want to say cynical, but just a little bit more serious,

203
00:10:39.940 --> 00:10:40.420
I think.

204
00:10:40.600 --> 00:10:42.940
But that's not the direction Aaron ended up going.

205
00:10:42.940 --> 00:10:50.580
But that's very interesting to be there, you know, at the introduction of a new character as and to see like how, you know, the writers,

206
00:10:50.780 --> 00:10:55.520
the director and actors sort of figured out this new person in the office.

207
00:10:56.280 --> 00:11:00.560
Do you remember when they told you, OK, we figured it out?

208
00:11:00.820 --> 00:11:02.340
We've picked an Aaron?

209
00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:03.280
Yeah.

210
00:11:03.480 --> 00:11:04.720
No, I think it was more.

211
00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:06.060
I think it was more gradual.

212
00:11:06.060 --> 00:11:11.820
And honestly, it's like, I don't know if it was just from talking with them in real life, because I do.

213
00:11:12.360 --> 00:11:18.580
Did you guys ever experience this where you'd be like talking with a writer with Mindy or BJ at the crafty and then all of a sudden you're like, wait a second.

214
00:11:18.760 --> 00:11:23.020
It's not exactly something you said, but something sort of related to what you said made its way into a script.

215
00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:24.260
I'm not taking credit for writing.

216
00:11:24.440 --> 00:11:30.060
I'm just saying that I think certain parts of my personality may have been incorporated into the aspects of Aaron.

217
00:11:30.060 --> 00:11:33.300
And we're like, oh, Ellie, we talk about this all the time.

218
00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:41.200
We totally say that one of the things we became aware of is having writers on the set, which is pretty unique and having them as cast members,

219
00:11:41.400 --> 00:11:43.820
that they were sort of always paying attention.

220
00:11:44.140 --> 00:11:49.860
And little moments of our real lives or things would end up in scripts very subtly, but they'd be there.

221
00:11:50.260 --> 00:11:50.460
Right.

222
00:11:50.900 --> 00:11:51.060
Right.

223
00:11:51.240 --> 00:11:55.480
And so then I'm like, oh, to be fly on the wall, that writer's room, like, what are they picking up?

224
00:11:55.480 --> 00:12:02.720
But also like checking what I say, I think I learned the lesson, not that it's a lesson, but I think I picked up on that too late where I was like, oh, right.

225
00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:07.840
Everything's material or everything's copy, whatever that quote is.

226
00:12:07.900 --> 00:12:08.540
But anyway, yeah.

227
00:12:08.860 --> 00:12:14.340
And I love, love how different her relationship with Michael is than Pam's relationship.

228
00:12:14.340 --> 00:12:16.060
And I thought that was really important.

229
00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:20.100
She brought out this whole other side of him and I loved it.

230
00:12:20.400 --> 00:12:24.220
I always thought of it as a father-daughter relationship.

231
00:12:24.220 --> 00:12:30.040
And, you know, Aaron didn't have a dad, a stable father, paternal figure growing up.

232
00:12:30.160 --> 00:12:35.000
And, you know, Michael, they have quirks that are similar, I think.

233
00:12:35.140 --> 00:12:35.860
And so they meshed.

234
00:12:35.940 --> 00:12:45.780
But yeah, Pam, like, I don't know if it's a wisdom or just like a ability to perceive that's a little sharper than Aaron's.

235
00:12:45.860 --> 00:12:49.280
But that was not the relationship you had with Michael.

236
00:12:49.480 --> 00:12:50.480
I mean, Pam had with Michael.

237
00:12:50.480 --> 00:12:55.340
